Subject: Register VM: a function with no formal parameter keeps its locals in registers when it reads `arguments`

What this does
  - quickjs.c regvm lowering (slot promotion map): the rule "a function with an `arguments` binding promotes no slot" now applies only
    when the function has formal parameters (`fd->arguments_var_idx < 0 || fd->arg_count == 0`). A function without a formal parameter
    promotes its locals as any other function does, provided it has a variable besides the `arguments` binding and the `this` copy (the forwarding wrapper `function(){ this.f.apply(this, arguments) }` keeps its frame so the fast region accepts it). The arguments slot itself (`arguments_var_idx` / `arguments_arg_idx`) stays out of
    the map exactly as before.

Why we need it
  The earleyboyer benchmark's `sc_list` and `sc_append` read `arguments.length` and `arguments[i]` only; the engine elides the object, but
  every other local (`res`, `i`) stayed in the frame's variable area, so the AOT generator could not make them frameless and each call
  built a frame (D lever, reviewer MSG 658-660).

Soundness
  A sloppy-mode arguments object aliases only the FORMAL parameter slots (js_build_mapped_arguments covers min(argc, arg_count) entries);
  it never aliases a local variable. With arg_count == 0 nothing is mapped, so no write through `arguments` can reach a promoted local.
  Slots a closure captures stay out of the map (js_u3_slot_private: is_captured), as do slots of functions with eval/with (has_eval_call,
  var_object_idx) and the slots named by get_arg_el / apply_arguments / make_loc_ref and the other opcodes the scan zeroes; none of that is
  touched. A function with parameters is unchanged.

Failure modes
  The digest of every parameterless function that reads `arguments` changes (its register code differs), so a profile recorded before the
  patch has no record for it. The interpreter's behaviour for such functions is unchanged except that the locals live in registers.

